From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Onyx contributors <onyx@localhost>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 22:33:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/16] USE(SKIA_GPU): Skia's GPU paths (Ganesh over GL / EGL)
 can be compiled out

What: a flag USE(SKIA_GPU) (wtf/PlatformUse.h: 1 by default with USE(SKIA); a port sets
USE_SKIA_GPU to 0), and under it everything of WebCore's Skia code that needs GL, EGL or Ganesh:
the whole of PlatformDisplaySkia.cpp, ImageBufferSkiaAcceleratedBackend, SkiaGPUAtlas,
SkiaReplayAtlas and SkiaReplayCanvas; in GraphicsContextSkia the GL context and promise-image
branches, createAcceleratedSurface(), the texture-backed tile of drawPattern() and the
recording entry points; the texture-backed paths of NativeImageSkia and ImageUtilitiesSkia; the
GPU helpers of SkiaUtilities; the fence of SkiaSerializedImageBuffer and of SkiaRecordingResult;
the GL / Ganesh includes and the accelerated case of ImageBuffer::create (a CPU-only build falls
through to the bitmap back end); PlatformDisplay's Skia members; Pattern::createPlatformPattern
without its GrContextThreadSafeProxy argument.

Why: every Skia port composites and may raster with GL, and USE(SKIA) code assumed it. A port
that renders on the CPU only (Onyx: no GL, a Skia library built without Ganesh) could neither
compile these files (no GL / EGL headers) nor link them (no Ganesh symbols). The run-time paths
were already CPU-safe (RenderingMode::Unaccelerated). With USE_SKIA_GPU at 1 nothing changes.
Assumes WebGL, Coordinated Graphics and GBM are off when it is 0 (they use GL under their own
flags).
